---
description: Keep bounded tasks local and delegate independent work to the best available agent profiles.
alwaysApply: true
---

Classify the work before calling `Task`:

- One bounded deliverable, a read-only lookup, or serial integration stays with the current/root agent. Do not delegate merely because the task touches files, spans several files, has tests, or a matching profile exists.
- For multiple genuinely independent deliverables, start every useful branch in one parallel batch. Choose the number of branches from the independent outcomes and available runtime capacity; do not use a fixed agent count. Root may call matching workers directly when the branches are clear. Use `orchestrator` when decomposition, dependencies, or joining results needs its own owner.
- Use `start` only when the user explicitly requests it (for example `/start`) or the host explicitly routes the request there. `start` sends one bounded deliverable directly to its best worker; it uses `orchestrator` only for genuinely independent branches or unclear decomposition.

Select exact profile IDs from the active agent catalog. Match each independent branch to the profile whose stated expertise fits it. When a marketplace/skill-backed profile matches, call that profile by its exact catalog name so its attached skill and identity are used; do not substitute a generic worker or merely ask one to load the same skill. Agents may delegate a new, independent subtask when this reduces the critical path and the runtime allows it. The active catalog and runtime admission are authoritative; never invent profiles or concurrency capacity.

Give each branch one owner, a distinct outcome, only the context it needs, non-overlapping write paths, and a checkable acceptance condition. The owner handles its own focused investigation, implementation, and narrow checks end to end. Add a separate researcher only when a read-only answer is itself a deliverable or a concrete unknown blocks execution. Launch independent branches together and join their results once.

After the join, run the acceptance checks needed for the request and review only when risk warrants it. Reopen work only for a concrete failed criterion or reproducible defect. Make one focused repair attempt, rerun only affected checks, and do not restart successful branches or repeat completed checks. A successful result ends the delegation. For partial/blocked work, name the actual blocker and remaining work; do not invent another owner or wave when nothing actionable remains.

If a Task launch is rejected before the child starts because its profile, account/model connection, or permissions are unavailable, treat that exact launch as blocked. Do not repeat the same call, substitute a generic profile, or launch the same child directly from root after a `start` task. Report the exact profile and connection/permission error; retry only after the relevant configuration or selection changes.

Follow the runtime's active concurrency, nesting, cancellation, and permission limits; prompts do not raise those limits. Never add a child task just to deepen or measure a Task chain. Synthesize verified results and distinguish confirmed facts from unknowns.
